{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Every commandment, etc.</b> One needs to know what commandment this is, and further, has He not already said ״עֵקֶב תִּשְׁמְעוּן וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם וַעֲשִׂיתֶם״, so what place is there to go back and say ״כָּל הַמִּצְוָה״? Indeed, the man Moses understood the hearts of the children of men — that a single spirit can lead astray small and great alike, and it is a ladder toward diminishing the service of HaShem and straying from the path of life. This is so: when a person performs two or three of HaShem's commandments that happen to come his way — how much more if he holds fast to one of all HaShem's commandments with constancy and zeal — there is born in him an evil disposition to grow lazy in the rest of the commandments that come to hand. And how much more if several commandments are in their hands, so that their soul is filled with a portion of the commandments. And this affliction spreads exceedingly, especially in the heart of those who walk in the Torah of HaShem — to treat the lighter commandments with contempt when they see that they are students of Torah and fulfill most of the commandments, etc. And this is what surrounds people with afflictions, and diminishes their honor, and multiplies their sorrows, and brings low man and casts down person. And therefore the prophet of HaShem came with his reproofs and said <b>כָּל הַמִּצְוָה</b> — attributing the entirety of the Torah to a single commandment, and commanding concerning all of it, that one not go astray in the aforementioned delusion." "<b>And He gave</b> a reason against which there is no reply, and said <b>לְמַעַן תִּחְיוּן וּבָאתֶם</b> etc. He intended in the statement <b>תִּחְיוּן</b> according to what they said, of blessed memory (Zohar I 170b): for in man there are 248 limbs and 365 sinews, and corresponding to them the word of HaShem came to guard 365 negative commandments and 248 positive commandments. Now, one can imagine a person in whom a pain or ache befalls one limb or sinew — his heart cries out in 'oy' and 'avoy' from its pain. Would he accept an answer to his cry if told: 'Do you not have 248 limbs, of which 247 are sound, and if a part of the 248 is ill, it is not fitting to accept this?' And this is the analogy for the guarding of the commandments, which are uniquely paired against the limbs and sinews: when a person transgresses and nullifies one of them, he activates pain in its counterpart among the limbs and sinews. And just as the sound health of 248 limbs is of no avail to remove the sensation of pain in the one limb that completes the count of 248, so too 247 positive commandments will not make up for the missing one to complete 248. And likewise, the guarding of 364 negative commandments will not make up for one nullified commandment to complete 365. And this is the statement <b>כָּל הַמִּצְוָה</b> — meaning, in all its totality, that nothing of what is in your power to do should be lacking — <b>תִּשְׁמְרוּן לַעֲשׂוֹת</b>, meaning, both negative commandments and positive commandments. And the reason <b>לְמַעַן תִּחְיוּן</b>: this depends on that — when you lack one of all the commandments, you lack, corresponding to it, the vitality of one limb." "And if you say that the analogy differs from the thing analogized — for in the analogy, the sensation of one limb is because it pains the entire unity of the body, whereas the commandments are not so — for this the prophet's statement came in this language: <b>כָּל הַמִּצְוָה</b>, meaning that the commandments of the Torah are also reckoned as one, even though they are many in number; they have one foundation and one structure. From this point on, they are exactly equivalent to the analogy alluded to." "And His saying <b>וּרְבִיתֶם</b>, meaning that through this medium there will be nothing preventing the blessings from reaching them, and they will live and multiply." "And His saying <b>וּבָאתֶם</b>: here he sounded a great alarm. For who among us is greater in commandments and in the service of HaShem than Moses, the faithful one, may he rest in peace? And go out and learn that through one matter his entry into the land was forfeited, and he was prevented from entering there even after death. And this is your sign: one must keep and perform every commandment in completeness. And aside from our approach, it further intends in the statement <b>תִּשְׁמְרוּן לַעֲשׂוֹת</b> to say that a person must sit and await when a commandment will come to his hand in order to perform it." "<b>Further</b>, it may be explained according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory, who said (Mo'ed Katan 28a): children, life, and sustenance do not depend on merit but on the constellation — end quote. And Moses' statement here comes to them with something new: that if Israel strengthen themselves to observe and perform all the commandments in their entirety, they are guaranteed all three of them together, and that is His saying <b>all the commandment</b> etc., <b>that you may live</b> — here is life; <b>and you shall multiply</b> — here are children; <b>and you shall come and possess</b> etc. — here is sustenance, the land and its fullness and houses filled with every good etc. From now on, anyone who strengthens himself in the perfection of Torah and commandments is guaranteed children, life, and sustenance. And this is your sign: Abraham, for whom the constellation indicated that he would not have children — yet because he strengthened himself in perfection of the service of HaShem, the constellation changed and he had children."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:17.819413110Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}